ed to have zero uncovered squares in a row (i.e., all squares covered), the requirement “each row and each column has exactly one uncovered unit square’’ would be violated. Hence the configuration with “one = 0’’ does **not** satisfy the problem constraints.\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nDenote the rows and columns of the grid by the index set \\([n]=\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 1. Structure of the uncovered squares  \n\nBecause each row must contain exactly one uncovered unit square and each column must also contain exactly one uncovered unit square, the set of uncovered squares is a set  \n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\pi(i))\\mid i\\in[n]\\},\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\pi:[n]\\to[n]\\) is a permutation.  \nConsequently the number of uncovered squares is \\(|U|=n\\); all other squares are covered.\n\nLet \\(C=[n]\\times[n]\\setminus U\\) be the set of covered squares.\n\n---\n\n#### 2. Matrix representation  \n\nDefine the \\(n\\times n\\) matrices  \n\n\\[\nJ=(j_{ij})\\quad\\text{with }j_{ij}=1\\ \\forall i,j,\\qquad \nI=(\\delta_{ij})\\quad\\text{with }\\delta_{ij}=1\\text{ iff }i=j .\n\\]\n\nThe indicator matrix of the covered squares is precisely  \n\n\\[\nM:=J-I .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, \\(M_{ij}=1\\) exactly when \\((i,j)\\notin U\\), i.e. when the cell \\((i,j)\\) is covered.\n\n---\n\n#### 3. Tiles as rank‑1 binary matrices  \n\nA rectangular tile \\(T\\) is determined by a non‑empty set of rows \\(R\\subseteq[n]\\) and a non‑empty set of columns \\(C\\subseteq[n]\\).  \nAll squares of the tile are the Cartesian product \\(R\\times C\\).  \n\nSince a tile may not contain any uncovered square, we must have  \n\n\\[\nR\\cap C=\\varnothing .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(\\mathbf{1}_R\\) (resp. \\(\\mathbf{1}_C\\)) be the column vector of length \\(n\\) that has entry 1 exactly on the indices belonging to \\(R\\) (resp. \\(C\\)).  \nThe indicator matrix of tile \\(T\\) is  \n\n\\[\nM_T=\\mathbf{1}_R\\mathbf{1}_C^{\\mathsf T}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nMatrix \\(M_T\\) is binary and has real rank 1 (it is an outer product of two non‑zero vectors).\n\n---\n\n#### 4. Lower bound on the number of tiles  \n\nAssume a tiling of the covered squares uses \\(k\\) tiles \\(T_1,\\dots ,T_k\\).  \nBecause the tiles are disjoint and together cover exactly the set \\(C\\), we have  \n\n\\[\nM=\\sum_{t=1}^{k}M_{T_t}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nTaking ranks and using subadditivity of rank,\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(M)\\le\\sum_{t=1}^{k}\\operatorname{rank}(M_{T_t})\n\\le k .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nWe now compute \\(\\operatorname{rank}(M)\\).  \nThe matrix \\(J\\) has eigenvalues \\(n\\) (once) and \\(0\\) (with multiplicity \\(n-1\\)).  \nThus the eigenvalues of \\(J-I\\) are \\(n-1\\) (once) and \\(-1\\) (with multiplicity \\(n-1\\)).  \nAll eigenvalues are non‑zero, so \\(J-I\\) is invertible and  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(J-I)=n .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nFrom (5)–(6) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge n .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nHence any tiling satisfying the problem’s conditions needs at least \\(n\\) tiles.\n\n---\n\n#### 5. Construction with exactly \\(n\\) tiles  \n\nFor each column index \\(j\\in[n]\\) define  \n\n\\[\nR_j=[n]\\setminus\\{j\\},\\qquad C_j=\\{j\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(j\\notin R_j\\), condition (2) holds, so the rectangle \\(R_j\\times C_j\\) is a legal tile.  \nIts size is \\((n-1)\\times1\\); it covers precisely the cells \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\).\n\nThe \\(n\\) tiles \\(\\{R_j\\times C_j\\mid j\\in[n]\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint (they occupy distinct columns) and their union is  \n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{j=1}^{n} (R_j\\times C_j)=\\{(i,j)\\mid i\\neq j\\}=C .\n\\]\n\nThus they cover all required squares and use exactly \\(n\\) tiles.\n\n---\n\n#### 6. Minimality  \n\nThe lower bound (7) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(n\\) tiles, while the construction above achieves \\(n\\) tiles.  \nTherefore the minimum possible number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2025}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 7. Remark on the “one = 0’’ scenario  \n\n