e disjoint and whose union consists of all unit squares of the \\(n\\times n\\) board except a set \\(H\\) of uncovered squares.  \nThe condition *“each row and each column has exactly one uncovered square”* means that  \n\n\\[\nH=\\{(i,p(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nfor some permutation \\(p\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); each row \\(i\\) contains the uncovered square \\((i,p(i))\\) and each column \\(j\\) contains the uncovered square \\((p^{-1}(j),j)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1. Reduction to the identity permutation  \n\nPermuting the columns of the board does not change the number of tiles needed: a rectangle \\([r_1,r_2]\\times[c_1,c_2]\\) is transformed into \\([r_1,r_2]\\times[\\sigma(c_1),\\sigma(c_2)]\\) where \\(\\sigma\\) is the column permutation.  \nChoose \\(\\sigma=p^{-1}\\); after this column permutation the uncovered squares become  \n\n\\[\nH'=\\{(i,i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nthe main diagonal.  \nConsequently it suffices to solve the problem for the diagonal holes; the obtained minimum will be the same for any permutation.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2. An explicit covering with \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nR_j^{\\text{up}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le j-1\\},\n\\]\n\na vertical rectangle of size \\((j-1)\\times1\\).  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nR_j^{\\text{down}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid j+1\\le i\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\na vertical rectangle of size \\((n-j)\\times1\\).\n\nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{T}= \\{R_j^{\\text{up}}\\mid 2\\le j\\le n\\}\\cup\\{R_j^{\\text{down}}\\mid 1\\le j\\le n-1\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) rectangles.  \nEvery cell \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i<j\\) belongs to \\(R_j^{\\text{up}}\\); every cell with \\(i>j\\) belongs to \\(R_j^{\\text{down}}\\).  \nThe diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\) belong to none of the rectangles.  \nThe rectangles are pairwise disjoint because each column appears in at most one of the two families and, within a family, the row intervals are disjoint.  \nThus \\(\\mathcal{T}\\) tiles the board except the diagonal, using exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3. A lower bound of \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nConsider the set  \n\n\\[\nS=\\bigl\\{(i,i+1)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\bigr\\}\\cup\n   \\bigl\\{(i,i-1)\\mid 2\\le i\\le n\\bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nGeometrically, \\(S\\) consists of the squares immediately to the right of each diagonal hole (except the rightmost column) together with the squares immediately to the left of each diagonal hole (except the leftmost column).  \nClearly \\(|S|= (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\).\n\n*Claim.* No axis‑parallel rectangle can contain two distinct squares of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof of the claim.*  \nTake two different squares of \\(S\\). There are three possibilities.\n\n1. Both belong to the “right‑hand” family, say \\((i,i+1)\\) and \\((j,j+1)\\) with \\(i<j\\).  \n   The smallest rectangle containing them has row interval \\([i,j]\\) and column interval \\([i+1,j+1]\\).  \n   Since \\(i+1\\le j\\), the cell \\((i+1,i+1)\\) lies inside this rectangle; \\((i+1,i+1)\\) is a diagonal hole, contradicting the requirement that a tile may not cover a hole.\n\n2. Both belong to the “left‑hand” family, say \\((i,i-1)\\) and \\((j,j-1)\\) with \\(i<j\\).  \n   The minimal rectangle has rows \\([i,j]\\) and columns \\([i-1,j-1]\\).  \n   Because \\(i\\le j-1\\), the cell \\((i,i)\\) (a diagonal hole) lies inside, again a contradiction.\n\n3. One square is from the right‑hand family and the other from the left‑hand family, say \\((i,i+1)\\) and \\((j,j-1)\\) with \\(i\\le j\\).  \n   Their minimal rectangle has rows \\([i,j]\\) and columns \\([i+1,j-1]\\).  \n   If \\(i+1\\le j-1\\) then the diagonal cell \\((i+1,i+1)\\) is inside; otherwise the rectangle collapses to a single column or ro