y tiling of the whole\n   board needs at least  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n   \\]\n\n   tiles.\n\n6. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   Choose the permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\) (the main diagonal).  \n   For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place a tile covering the *right* part of row \\(i\\):\n   the rectangle \\(\\{(i,j):j=i+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (size \\(1\\times (n-i)\\)).  \n   For each \\(j=2,\\dots ,n\\) place a tile covering the *left* part of column\n   \\(j\\):\n   the rectangle \\(\\{(i,j):i=j+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (size \\((n-j)\\times 1\\)).  \n\n   These are exactly \\(n-1\\) row\u2011tiles and \\(n-1\\) column\u2011tiles, altogether\n   \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  They are pairwise disjoint and together cover every square\n   except the diagonal holes \\((i,i)\\).  Thus the bound is attainable.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   The minimum number of tiles required is \\(2n-2\\).  With \\(n=2025\\),\n\n   \\[\n   2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\n   \\]\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nA placement of tiles satisfying the condition \u201ceach row and each column has\nexactly one uncovered unit square\u2019\u2019 forces the uncovered squares to be a set  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\pi(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\pi\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nIndeed, each row \\(i\\) contains precisely one uncovered square, which we denote\nby \\((i,\\pi(i))\\); the column indices \\(\\pi(i)\\) are all distinct because each\ncolumn also contains exactly one uncovered square.\n\nFor a fixed row \\(i\\) the covered squares split into two intervals\n\n\\[\nL_i=\\{(i,j):j<\\pi(i)\\},\\qquad\nR_i=\\{(i,j):j>\\pi(i)\\}.\n\\]\n\nA rectangle whose interior contains a square of \\(L_i\\) cannot contain a square\nof \\(R_i\\), for otherwise it would contain the hole \\((i,\\pi(i))\\).  Hence any\ntiling of the board consists of two disjoint families of rectangles:\n\n* rectangles that lie entirely inside \\(\\bigcup_i L_i\\) (the *left* part);\n* rectangles that lie entirely inside \\(\\bigcup_i R_i\\) (the *right* part).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A lower bound for the left part  \n\nRe\u2011index the rows so that the hole\u2011columns increase:\n\n\\[\n\\pi(r_1)<\\pi(r_2)<\\dots <\\pi(r_n).\n\\]\n\nIn this order the left part is the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\bigcup_{k=1}^{n}\\{(r_k,j):1\\le j<\\pi(r_k)\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(k\\) define the *row\u2011length*  \n\n\\[\nL_k=\\pi(r_k)-1 .\n\\]\n\nBecause the \\(\\pi(r_k)\\) are distinct, the positive numbers among\n\\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\) are all distinct; exactly one of them equals \\(0\\)\n(the row whose hole lies in column\u202f1).  Consequently \\(\\mathcal L\\) is a Young\ndiagram with precisely \\(n-1\\) distinct row\u2011lengths.\n\n**Claim.** A Young diagram with \\(t\\) distinct row\u2011lengths cannot be partitioned\ninto fewer than \\(t\\) axis\u2011parallel rectangles.\n\n*Proof of the claim.*  \nConsider any rectangle \\(R\\) inside the diagram.  Let \\(m\\) be the smallest row\nindex occurring in \\(R\\).  All columns of \\(R\\) must lie to the left of the hole\nin row \\(m\\); therefore the width of \\(R\\) is at most the row\u2011length of row\n\\(m\\).  Consequently every rectangle is confined to a single row\u2011length value.\nIf two different rows have different lengths, a rectangle covering a square in\nthe longer row cannot also contain a square in the shorter row.  Hence each\ndistinct row\u2011length forces a distinct rectangle, proving the claim. \u220e\n\nApplying the claim to \\(\\mathcal L\\) yields that at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are\nnecessary to cover the left part.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A lower bound for the right part  \n\nPerform the same construction with the rows ordered by decreasing hole\u2011column,\ni.e. \\(\\pi(s_1)>\\pi(s_2)>\\dots >\\pi(s_n)\\).  The right part becomes a Young\ndiagram whose row\u2011lengths are  \n\n\\[\nR_k=n-\\pi(s_k),\\qquad k=1,\\dots ,n .\n\\]\n\nAgain the \\(R_k\\) are all distinct, with exactly one equal to \\(0\\) (the row\nwhose hole lies in column\u202f\\(n\\)).  By the same claim, covering the right part\nrequires at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Combined lower bound  \n\nSince a rectangle cannot intersect both the left and the right part, the total\nnumber of rectangles in any tiling is at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  A tiling that meets the bound  \n\nChoose the permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\) (the main diagonal).  \nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place the rectangle  \n\n\\[\nT_i^{\\text{row}}=\\{(i,j):j=i+1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich covers the whole right side of row \\(i\\).  \nFor each \\(j=2,\\dots ,n\\) place the rectangle  \n\n\\[\nT_j^{\\text{col}}=\\{(i,j):i=j+1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich covers the whole left side of column \\(j\\).\n\nThe sets \\(\\{T_i^{\\text{row}}\\}_{i=1}^{n-1}\\) and\n\\(\\{T_j^{\\text{col}}\\}_{j=2}^{n}\\) are pairwise disjoint; together they cover\nevery square of the board except the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\), which are the\n\\(n\\) uncovered squares required by the problem.  The number of tiles used is\n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Minimality  \n\nInequality (1) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles, while\nconstruction (2) shows that \\(2n-2\\) tiles are sufficient.  Hence the minimum\npossible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\nWith \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = \\boxed{4048}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Thus Matilda can achieve the required condition with exactly 4048 rectangular\ntiles, and it is impossible to do it with fewer.**"
