uct of the row interval \\([i,k]\\) and the column interval \\([i-1,k-1]\\); in particular it contains the cell \\((i,k-1)\\).  Since \\(k-1\\ge i\\), the column index of this cell exceeds the allowed bound \\(j<i\\) for row \\(i\\); thus \\((i,k-1)\\notin L\\), contradicting \\(Q\\subseteq L\\).  ∎\n\n   Because there are exactly \\(n-1\\) cells \\(d_i\\) in \\(L\\), the lemma implies that covering \\(L\\) by rectangles requires at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.  The same argument applied to the symmetric staircase \\(R\\) (using the cells \\((i,i+1),\\,i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\)) shows that at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed for \\(R\\) as well.\n\n5. **Combined lower bound.**  \n   Since the tiles covering \\(L\\) and those covering \\(R\\) are disjoint, any tiling of the whole board uses at least  \n   \\[\n   (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n   \\]\n   tiles.\n\n6. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   Choose the permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\) (holes on the main diagonal).  \n   - For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place the **row tile**  \n     \\[\n     T_i^{\\text{row}}=\\{(i,j):j=i+1,\\dots ,n\\},\n     \\]\n     a rectangle of size \\(1\\times (n-i)\\) covering the entire right part of row \\(i\\).  \n   - For each \\(j=2,\\dots ,n\\) place the **column tile**  \n     \\[\n     T_j^{\\text{col}}=\\{(i,j):i=j+1,\\dots ,n\\},\n     \\]\n     a rectangle of size \\((n-j)\\times 1\\) covering the entire left part of column \\(j\\).\n\n   The two families are pairwise disjoint; together they cover every square except the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\), which are precisely the required uncovered squares.  The total number of tiles is\n   \\[\n   (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n   \\]\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound \\(2n-2\\) and the explicit construction both show that the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).  With \\(n=2025\\),\n\n   \\[\n   2\\cdot2025-2 = \\boxed{4048}.\n   \\]\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  We work with the \\(n\\times n\\